<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

	<PropertyGroup>
		<TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
		<ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
		<Nullable>enable</Nullable>
		<Authors>LostBeard</Authors>
		<RepositoryType>git</RepositoryType>
		<PackageReadmeFile>README.md</PackageReadmeFile>
		<PackageLicenseFile>LICENSE.txt</PackageLicenseFile>
		<PackageIcon>icon-128.png</PackageIcon>
		<GenerateDocumentationFile>true</GenerateDocumentationFile>
		<!-- 🔴 With this on, `dotnet BUILD -c Release` makes the nupkg. `dotnet pack` does NOT rebuild: it repackages the last
		     build's DLL, so a version bump never reaches the binary (2026-11-01: 1.4.0-local.5 shipped a DLL stamped
		     1.4.0-local.4+1cc937c). Build, then check the packed DLL's InformationalVersion. -->
		<GeneratePackageOnBuild>True</GeneratePackageOnBuild>
		<EmbedAllSources>true</EmbedAllSources>
		<DebugType>Embedded</DebugType>
		<LangVersion>latest</LangVersion>
		<IsPackable>true</IsPackable>
		<PackageProjectUrl>https://github.com/LostBeard/SpawnDev.AI</PackageProjectUrl>
		<RepositoryUrl>https://github.com/LostBeard/SpawnDev.AI.git</RepositoryUrl>
		<PackageTags>AI;LLM;Ollama;DotNetWebAssembly;WebGPU;GGUF;ToolCalling</PackageTags>
		<Title>SpawnDev.AI.Server</Title>
		<Version>1.4.5</Version>
		<PackageReleaseNotes>v1.4.5 - On SpawnDev.ILGPU.ML 5.8.11: faster batch-decoder decode (CUDA, qwen3:1.7b-q8_0 on an RTX 4070: 166.9 -> 182.3 tok/s at a short prompt, 141.3 -> 174.1 at 1,602 tokens; greedy text unchanged), the v projection overlapped with q/k on CUDA (graph fork/join). v1.4.4 - On SpawnDev.ILGPU.ML 5.8.6: the batch decoder regroups Q8_0 weights in place at load (same bytes, no extra memory), so Q8_0 models decode about 13% faster in the browser on WebGPU (Qwen3-1.7B Q8_0, RTX 4070: 147.4 -> 166.3 tok/s at a short prompt, measured by SpawnDev.ILGPU.ML). LoRA adapters over Q8_0 bases give the same tokens as 5.8.5. v1.4.3 - On SpawnDev.ILGPU.ML 5.8.5: Q8_0 GGUF models decode about 2x faster on CUDA through the batch decoder (qwen3:1.7b-q8_0 on an RTX 4070: 82.5 -> 164.0 tok/s at a short prompt, 71.9 -> 140.8 at 1,600 prompt tokens, greedy output unchanged), a faster Q6_K decode GEMV, a per-run output leak fixed in 23 pipelines, and an in-place LoRA adapter swap on a resident batch-decoder model now takes effect on the next decode (recorded decode steps are dropped). v1.4.2 - On SpawnDev.ILGPU.ML 5.8.3 and SpawnDev.ILGPU 5.4.16: faster tensor-core prefill GEMMs and flash prefill (ldmatrix) on the batch decoder, LoRA training steps 2.5x faster (llama3.1:8b 4.5 -> 1.78 s on an RTX 4070). v1.4.1 - AiChatEngine.PassObserver: each generation pass with its exact rendered prompt (token ids and text with special tokens kept), generated token ids, end-of-turn ids, the stop token that ended it, the LoRA adapter label and raw output, for training data that matches inference; AiChatEngine.TokenizeAsync / DecodeAsync; LoRA adapters: ModelRegistry.SetAdapterAsync (PEFT directory or LoraAdapterSet, either engine, swapped in place on the batch decoder). On SpawnDev.ILGPU.ML 5.8.2. v1.4.0 - AiMemoryEngine: memory search on the accelerator (all-MiniLM-L6-v2 + DeviceVectorIndex; 50,000 entries p95 5.6 ms on an RTX 4070 including the embed) and ConsolidateAsync (episodes into facts with provenance, conflicts superseded). OllamaModelInstaller (install a model without Ollama, models.spawndev.com then registry.ollama.ai). AiFaceEngine: face detection and recognition (YuNet + SFace) on the shared accelerator, with a face registry (introductions, Visitor N guests, expiry). Chat on the batch decoder by default (GgufEngine.Auto: qwen2 / qwen3 / llama; llama3.1:8b on an RTX 4070 at a 3.7k-token prompt: first token after 200 new tokens 204 ms, prompt 1,660 tok/s, decode 45.5 tok/s; tensor-core prefill on CUDA sm_75+). Ollama /api/chat and /api/generate report eval_count / eval_duration and the other timing fields. AiSpeakerEngine.ReleaseWorkingMemoryAsync. On SpawnDev.ILGPU.ML 5.8.1 and SpawnDev.ILGPU 5.4.15. v1.3.0 - Live words: AiStreamingSpeechEngine (Nemotron Speech Streaming 0.6B, words as they are spoken, 5.63% WER on 60 LibriSpeech clips, a 160 ms chunk in ~70 ms). Full history: CHANGELOG.md</PackageReleaseNotes>
		<Description>Ollama/OpenAI-compatible model server on the SpawnDev.ILGPU.ML inference engine (GGUF, KV-cache decode, WebGPU dispatch-plan capture/replay). One handler core, two transports: HTTP on desktop (drop-in for Ollama clients on :11434), MessagePort in the browser (shared/dedicated worker) - the same request/response shapes either way.</Description>
	</PropertyGroup>

	<ItemGroup>
		<ProjectReference Include="..\SpawnDev.AI\SpawnDev.AI.csproj" />
	</ItemGroup>
	
	<ItemGroup>
		<None Include="..\icon-128.png" Pack="true" PackagePath="\" />
		<None Include="..\LICENSE.txt" Pack="true" PackagePath="\" />
		<None Include="..\README.md" Pack="true" PackagePath="\" />
	</ItemGroup>

	<ItemGroup>
		<PackageReference Include="SpawnDev.ILGPU.ML" Version="5.8.11" />
		<!-- Direct reference: NuGet resolves the LOWEST version satisfying ML's floor (5.3.4 asks for
		     SpawnDev.ILGPU >= 5.3.5), so an ILGPU fix released after the ML package reaches the server and the
		     demo only through this line. Keep it at the newest ILGPU. -->
		<PackageReference Include="SpawnDev.ILGPU" Version="5.4.24" />
		<PackageReference Include="SpawnDev.SpawnJS.WebWorkers" Version="2.2.5" />
	</ItemGroup>

</Project>
